            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "At 17 cents per kWh and 12 hours a day, a typical ultrasonic costs about $2 a month in electricity, an evaporative about $4, and a warm mist unit about $18. Use watts × hours ÷ 1,000 × your rate to check your own model. Add filters, cartridges or distilled water, which can cost more than the power."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do humidifiers use a lot of electricity?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Ultrasonic and evaporative humidifiers use little, typically 20 to 100 watts, similar to a light bulb or small fan. Warm mist and steam humidifiers use far more, typically 200 to 500 watts for room units, because boiling water takes about 2.7 kWh per gallon. If electricity cost matters, avoid the boiling types."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the most energy efficient humidifier?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Ultrasonic humidifiers draw the least power, followed closely by evaporative models. Both avoid boiling water, which is where most humidifier energy goes. The most efficient setup is either type controlled by a humidistat, so it runs only until the room reaches its target and then stops, rather than running on high all day."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much does a whole house humidifier cost installed?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Typical installed prices are roughly $400 to $900 for a bypass unit, $500 to $1,200 for a fan-powered unit and $1,000 to $2,500 for a steam unit. The final price depends on furnace access, whether a water line and drain are nearby, and the controls chosen. Get at least two written quotes."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much does a whole house humidifier cost to run?",
