          <p>
            Three things stand out. First, the ultrasonic on tap water with cartridges is the cheapest way to humidify a room, provided you can live with some white dust or your water is soft. Second, the same machine run on bottled distilled water becomes the most expensive portable option by a wide margin, entirely because of the water. Third, the warm mist unit that looked cheapest at the register ends up costing several times its purchase price in electricity.
          </p>

          <p>
            The evaporative console lands in the middle and is the most predictable: no white dust, no expensive water, a modest bill and a known wick cost. For a large room or an open plan, that predictability is often worth more than shaving another hundred dollars. The whole-house numbers cover the entire home rather than one room, so compare a bypass unit against two or three portable units, not one; on that basis it is competitive, and it removes the daily chore of refilling tanks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Steam whole-house systems are the premium choice for good reasons, mainly precise control and compatibility with heat pumps, but they carry a running cost to match. If your furnace burns gas and runs often in winter, a bypass or fan-powered unit delivers most of the benefit for a fraction of the operating cost.
          </p>
